<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Old Saybrook</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Old Saybrook is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut-river">Connecticut River</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/long-island-sound">Long Island Sound</a>. It grew from Saybrook Fort, built in 1635–1636 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-winthrop-the-younger">John Winthrop the Younger</a> for the Puritan holders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwick-patent">Warwick Patent</a> and named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-fiennes-1st-viscount-saye-and-sele">Lord Saye and Sele</a> and Lord Brooke. The settlement joined the Connecticut Colony in 1644, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yale-university">Yale College</a> was based there before moving to New Haven in 1716.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/old-saybrook.jpg" alt="Old Saybrook" width="320" height="216" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juliejules · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saybrook_breakwater_light.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
